# Integration plan — android-bike-radar-overlay

Goal: let Crazy Capy Randonneur consume live rear-radar data from the
[android-bike-radar-overlay](https://github.com/partymola/android-bike-radar-overlay)
app (package `es.jjrh.bikeradar`, GPL-3.0) when both are installed, so a
real Varia-class rear radar's targets, battery and tail-light state can be
shown and controlled from inside CrazyCapy.

The integration is two-sided: a small IPC surface added to the overlay app,
and a consumer + UI in CrazyCapy. This plan scopes the CrazyCapy side and
defines the wire contract. The two apps are developed separately and signed
differently, so the interface must be agreed as a *contract*, never copied
code (see licensing below).

android-bike-radar-overlay overlay is good and can be used but it would be
nice to be able to turn it on/off it might also be nice to be able to controll
backling with a button or maybe in the future automatic from sunset/sunrise data
and maybe weather date if that is included in the app.

It might also be nice to show cars behind you on the map view along the route
so you don't need to look at diffeernt places in the app whn focused following
a route and maybe closing in on a turn.

android-bike-radar-overlay has no IPC surface exists today: the foreground
service, all receivers, the FileProvider and dev services are `exported="false"`;
`MainActivity` is launcher-only. The only outbound data path is Home Assistant MQTT.

## Interface choice

**Decision: one exported bound service in the overlay app, exposed via AIDL.**

- Commands + queries (tail-light mode, overlay show/hide, battery %): plain
  AIDL methods with a result — intents cannot return a value.
- Streaming (targets at ~5 Hz): a registered `oneway` AIDL listener backed by
  `RemoteCallbackList`; the overlay app pushes snapshot parcelables and the
  consumer unregisters when its screen goes away. This is the native Android
  pattern (cf. `LocationManager`), parcel-based (no JSON/socket cost), and fits
  CrazyCapy's battery-extreme ethos.
- Intents (exported broadcast receivers) are rejected for the stream: no
  request/response, no backpressure, a broadcast per frame is heavier than a
  held binder channel. They remain an acceptable *fallback* for the two
  fire-and-forget commands only.

Rejected alternatives: localhost HTTP/WebSocket server in the overlay app
(extra socket + serialization per frame, weaker than a parcel, and needs the
server bound to `127.0.0.1`); a ContentProvider (fine for battery snapshots,
no streaming).

### Contract sketch (CrazyCapy side — written clean-room)

A question is if the interface should support multiple connected radars? 
e.g. should a "radar-id" be part of all calls in the API? (and a query/list also added).

AIDL interface + parcelables live in `app/src/aidl/.../radar/`:

```
IRadarService
  // snapshots
  int getBatteryPercent();                 // -1 = unknown
  boolean isConnected();
  // commands
  void setRadarLightMode(int mode);        // mode enum mirror of RadarLightMode type bytes
  void setOverlayVisible(boolean visible);
  // streaming
  void registerTargetListener(IRadarTargetListener l);
  void unregisterTargetListener(IRadarTargetListener l);
  boolean registerTargetListener(IRadarTargetListener l); // false if radar not present

oneway IRadarTargetListener
  void onRadarState(RadarStateParcel state);   // pushed at ~5 Hz while registered

parcelable RadarVehicleParcel { int id; int distanceM; int closingKmh;
  int size; float lateralPos; float rangeXm; boolean isBehind; }
parcelable RadarStateParcel { long timestamp; RadarVehicleParcel[] vehicles;
  float bikeSpeedMs; boolean isClear; }
```

Data shapes mirror `RadarState.kt` / `Vehicle` so the simulator used for the
map-layer work is drop-in replaceable by the real stream (see Phase 1).

## Security

- The two apps are signed by different keys, so a `signature`-level permission
  is impossible. Use a custom permission declared by the overlay app
  (`protectionLevel="dangerous"` or `normal`) that CrazyCapy requests via
  `<uses-permission>`; the user approves it at install.
- The overlay app must keep the service `exported="true"` **only** with that
  permission enforced (`android:permission="..."` on the service), so a random
  installed app cannot subscribe to your rear-radar stream or flip your light.
- Bind explicitly by component (`Intent.setComponent`), never action-based.

## Licensing

- Overlay app: **GPL-3.0-or-later**. CrazyCapy: **Apache-2.0**. These are not
  link-compatible; the two APKs stay separate processes and communicate over
  binder with no shared code.
- The AIDL contract, parcelables and any constants describing the wire format
  (UUIDs, mode type bytes, parcel field layout) are **factual interface data**
  and are written clean-room in CrazyCapy's repo. Do NOT copy the overlay
  repo's `.aidl`/`.kt` files or comments; re-derive field meanings from the
  published protocol or the values observed on the wire.
- No GPL code is imported into the CrazyCapy APK.

## Phase 0 — validate the UI with a simulator (DONE in this work)

Before any IPC work, prove the idea in-app using the existing ghost ride:

- Pure `sim.RadarSimulator` spawns fake cars/trucks/bikes 175 m behind the
  rider, faster than the rider's current speed, closing until they pass and
  then disappearing (a rear radar only looks back).
- Speed bands: cars/trucks 30–100 km/h, bikes 20–45 km/h; a target slower than
  or equal to the rider is never shown.
- Targets drawn on the map as colored dots behind the rider, driven from
  `RideStore.radarTargets`; toggle in the ghost-ride start popup, persisted in
  settings.
- Deliverable: a decision on whether the map is a sensible surface for rear
  radar info (lane/lateral resolution, range legibility at riding zoom).

## Phase 1 — consume the real stream

- Write the clean-room AIDL contract into the repo (above).
- Create an optional `RadarClient` (bind, subscribe, expose a `StateFlow` of
  radar state) that is inert when the overlay app is absent.
- Map the `RadarStateParcel` stream onto the same layer plumbing built in
  Phase 0 (rename sim types to a shared `radar.RadarVehicle` model).
- Decide whether the sim remains a debug-only fallback when the overlay app is
  missing.

## Phase 2 — battery + tail light + overlay control

- Add `getBatteryPercent` and `setRadarLightMode` to the HUD/settings UI
  (radar battery chip, tail-light off/on button).
- Add `setOverlayVisible` triggered from a CrazyCapy control if the overlay
  app exposes it.
- Maybe show a graceful "rear radar not available" state when the overlay
  app is expected/selectd but not installed or permission is not granted.

## Phase 3 — the overlay app's IPC surface

Owned by the overlay app's maintainer (not this repo): an exported,
permission-gated bound service implementing the agreed contract, plus the
`setOverlayVisible` hook. CrazyCapy only carries the client side.

## Status

- Phase 0: in progress (this change set).
- Phases 1–3: not started.
